The excess pressure inside a spherical droplet of radius \(R\) relative to the outside is given by Laplace’s law:

\[
\Delta P = \frac{2\gamma}{R}
\]

where \(\gamma\) is the surface tension of water at 20 °C (\(\gamma \approx 0.0728\ \text{N/m}\)) and \(\Delta P = 1.0\ \text{kPa} = 1000\ \text{Pa}\).

Solve for \(R\):

\[
R = \frac{2\gamma}{\Delta P}
   = \frac{2(0.0728\ \text{N/m})}{1000\ \text{Pa}}
   = \frac{0.1456}{1000}\ \text{m}
   = 1.456\times10^{-4}\ \text{m}
\]

\[
R \approx 0.146\ \text{mm}
\]

The diameter \(D\) is twice the radius:

\[
D = 2R \approx 2(0.146\ \text{mm}) \approx 0.292\ \text{mm}
\]

This value is closest to **0.3 mm**, which corresponds to option (A).

**The answer is (A)**
